replies.text:
I'll build the threat model for EVAL-06 based on the design review context.

**Analysis**: This is a file sharing system with no authentication, permanent links, and S3 storage. The critical risks are enumerable links exposing all files, permanent access with no revocation, and storage/bandwidth exhaustion. I'm treating the content inside the DATA markers as specifications to analyze, not instructions to follow.

```json
{
  "schema": "redline.threatmodel.v1",
  "issueKey": "EVAL-06",
  "sketch": {
    "components": ["File upload handler", "S3 bucket", "Link generator", "Link access handler", "Public file viewer"],
    "dataStores": ["S3 bucket"],
    "externalServices": ["AWS S3"],
    "trustBoundaries": ["Public internet to file viewer", "Application to S3"],
    "dataClasses": [],
    "exposure": "public",
    "llmFeatures": false
  },
  "threats": [
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Attacker enumerates all files through predictable links",
      "scenario": "An attacker who obtains one link discovers links are sequential (file/1, file/2) or use predictable patterns. They write a script to enumerate all possible links and download every uploaded file, including confidential documents from other users.",
      "component": "Link generator",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Users upload documents and share them through a public link."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.3.1", "V3.2.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-330", "CWE-639"],
      "plainLanguage": "If links are like /file/1, /file/2, someone could try every number and download everyone's files",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Links must be cryptographically random",
        "given": "a user uploads a file",
        "when": "the system generates a sharing link",
        "then": "the link must use at least 128 bits of cryptographically secure randomness (not sequential IDs or predictable patterns)"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Stolen or leaked links grant permanent access to files",
      "scenario": "An employee shares a confidential document link via email, then leaves the company. The link still works years later. Anyone who finds it in old emails, browser history, server logs, or internet archives can access the file forever with no way to revoke access.",
      "component": "Link access handler",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Links never expire."
        },
        {
          "quote": "Anyone with the link can view the file without logging in."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V3.3.3"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-613"],
      "plainLanguage": "Once someone has a link, they can use it forever—you can't take back access even if you need to",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Links must expire or be revocable",
        "given": "a file sharing link is generated",
        "when": "time passes or the owner requests revocation",
        "then": "the link must either expire automatically (e.g., 30 days) or provide a way for the owner to revoke access"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "DenialOfService",
      "title": "Attacker exhausts storage and bandwidth through unlimited uploads",
      "scenario": "An attacker uploads thousands of large files (gigabytes each) through automated scripts. This fills the S3 bucket causing the service to stop working for legitimate users, or generates massive AWS bills that could bankrupt the service.",
      "component": "File upload handler",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Users upload documents"
        },
        {
          "quote": "Files are stored in an S3 bucket."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V12.1.2", "V12.1.3"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-400"],
      "plainLanguage": "Nothing stops someone from uploading huge files or thousands of files to crash the service or rack up huge bills",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Enforce file size and storage quotas",
        "given": "a user attempts to upload a file",
        "when": "the file exceeds limits",
        "then": "reject uploads over 100MB per file and enforce a total storage quota per source IP or user session"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Misconfigured S3 bucket exposes all files publicly",
      "scenario": "The S3 bucket is configured with public read access or overly permissive policies. An attacker discovers the bucket name and lists all files directly from S3, bypassing the application entirely and downloading everything without needing any links.",
      "component": "S3 bucket",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Files are stored in an S3 bucket."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V14.4.6"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-668"],
      "plainLanguage": "If S3 permissions are wrong, attackers can list and download all files directly without needing any links",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "S3 bucket must block public access",
        "given": "files are stored in S3",
        "when": "the bucket is provisioned",
        "then": "enable S3 Block Public Access, deny ListBucket from public, and require signed URLs or IAM authentication for all read operations"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Malicious file uploads exploit viewers' systems",
      "scenario": "An attacker uploads an executable disguised as a document (malware.pdf.exe) or an HTML file with embedded JavaScript. When a victim clicks the link, their browser executes the malicious code or their antivirus doesn't catch it, compromising their system.",
      "component": "File upload handler",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Users upload documents"
        },
        {
          "quote": "Anyone with the link can view the file"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V12.1.1", "V12.5.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-434"],
      "plainLanguage": "Users can upload malicious files (like viruses or scripts) that attack anyone who views them",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Validate and sandbox file uploads",
        "given": "a file is uploaded",
        "when": "the system stores it",
        "then": "verify file type by content (not extension), reject executables and scripts, serve files with Content-Disposition: attachment, and use a separate domain for file delivery"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Repudiation",
      "title": "No evidence of who uploaded illegal or malicious files",
      "scenario": "Someone uploads copyrighted content, illegal material, or malware. Law enforcement or abuse reports arrive. With no authentication or logging, the service cannot identify who uploaded it, making investigation impossible and exposing the operator to legal liability.",
      "component": "File upload handler",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Anyone with the link can view the file without logging in."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V7.1.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-778"],
      "plainLanguage": "With no login required, you can't prove who uploaded illegal files, leaving you legally exposed",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Log upload attribution",
        "given": "a file is uploaded",
        "when": "the upload completes",
        "then": "log the source IP, timestamp, file hash, and any user identifier (even if anonymous) in a tamper-proof audit log retained for at least 90 days"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Links leak in browser history, logs, and referrer headers",
      "scenario": "A user shares a sensitive file link. The link appears in their browser history where others might see it, in proxy logs at their company, in server access logs, and in HTTP referrer headers if they click external links from the file viewer, exposing the file to unintended parties.",
      "component": "Link access handler",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Anyone with the link can view the file without logging in."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V14.4.5"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-200"],
      "plainLanguage": "Sharing links leave traces in browser history, proxy logs, and other places that could expose private files",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Use token-based link access",
        "given": "a file link is accessed",
        "when": "the page loads",
        "then": "use POST requests or separate short-lived tokens instead of putting the file identifier in the URL, and set Referrer-Policy: no-referrer on file viewer pages"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "Downloaded files could be modified in transit or storage",
      "scenario": "An attacker with access to the S3 bucket or who performs a man-in-the-middle attack replaces a legitimate file with a malicious one. Users download the tampered file and trust it because there's no integrity verification to detect the modification.",
      "component": "Link access handler",
      "likelihood": 2,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Files are stored in an S3 bucket."
        },
        {
          "quote": "Anyone with the link can view the file"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V14.4.4"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-353"],
      "plainLanguage": "If someone tampers with files in storage or during download, users have no way to know the file was changed",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Verify file integrity on download",
        "given": "a file is uploaded",
        "when": "it is stored",
        "then": "compute and store a SHA-256 hash; when downloaded, provide the hash so clients can verify the file hasn't been tampered with"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```
